# Check out EVERY text file with LF, on every platform.
#
# The 2026-10-09 CI run failed its Windows leg on exactly two
# targets, `add_output_golden` and `belt_join_golden`. Each compares a committed
# JSON fixture (crates/fabrico-command/tests/fixtures/*.expected.json) byte for
# byte, and the windows-latest runner checks out with core.autocrlf=true, so both
# fixtures arrived with CRLF. The repository's rule is identical bytes on every
# platform, so every text file is pinned to LF at checkout here rather than one
# extension at a time after each new failure. Files git detects as binary are
# never converted (`text=auto`), and the explicit `binary` rules below still win.
# The index already holds LF everywhere (`git ls-files --eol`: zero i/crlf, zero
# i/mixed), so this needs no renormalisation commit.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Force shell scripts to LF on EVERY platform's checkout.
#
# The determinism gate scripts (scripts/*.sh) are executed verbatim by the CI
# `determinism` job on the windows-latest runner via Git Bash (defaults.run.shell:
# bash). A CRLF checkout would inject `\r` into shebangs / `set -euo pipefail` and
# break the bash gate (RESEARCH Pitfall 3 / A2). Pinning *.sh to LF keeps the same
# bytes on Linux, macOS, and Windows so the gate runs unmodified everywhere.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Rust sources are text; let git normalize line endings on commit. (Not
# load-bearing for the gate, but keeps cross-platform diffs clean.)
*.rs text

# ── Everything below is LOAD-BEARING FOR DETERMINISM, not tidiness ──────────
#
# Any file embedded verbatim by `include_str!` / `include_bytes!` becomes part
# of a hashed starting state. Git's eol conversion rewrites bytes at CHECKOUT
# time, per platform, which means an unpinned embedded asset makes the same
# commit hash differently on different operating systems.
#
# This is not hypothetical. The first CI run that ever reached the Windows
# certification suite failed on it: `order_scene_regenerates_to_an_identical_
# hash_from_two_appliers` computed acd72356... where the Linux-pinned anchor is
# 40c167ec.... The windows-latest runner checks out with core.autocrlf=true, so
# `crates/fabrico-host/src/scenes/order_processing.ron` arrived with CRLF, and
# its three raw multi-line JS factory bodies (`Configure(node, config:
# Factory(source: r#"..."#))`, lines 98/104/110) carried the \r into the edit
# log. Reproduced locally by converting that one file to CRLF, which yields the
# Windows hash exactly.
#
# Note what did NOT fail: both appliers on Windows agreed with each other, and
# every golden-corpus fixture passed. The core is deterministic across
# platforms. What diverged was the bytes git handed it.

# Scene definitions are embedded by `include_str!` (demo_scene.rs:113/119/134)
# and their raw string bodies are hashed byte-verbatim.
*.ron text eol=lf

# The golden corpus manifests are embedded by `include_str!`
# (cert_cross_platform.rs). They tolerate CRLF today because the parser trims,
# but that tolerance is an accident of the parser, not a guarantee.
crates/fabrico-core/tests/golden/**/hashes.txt text eol=lf
crates/fabrico-core/tests/golden/README.md text eol=lf

# The frozen corpus payloads are embedded by `include_bytes!` and must reach the
# decoder as the exact bytes that were blessed. Git currently leaves them alone
# because its NUL-byte heuristic guesses "binary"; declaring it removes the
# guess. `binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff`: never convert, never munge.
*.postcard binary
*.snapshot binary
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
